{
  "configuration_id": "config_049",
  "reason": "Evidence: The strongest performance driver identified so far is the RNA encoder choice—switching from pca_n2000 to scvi_n200 in an otherwise identical setup (resnet50, avgpool, weighted, multiprofile) yielded a ~4% accuracy gain (0.639→0.678). The current best configuration (config_037: scvi_n200 + imagenet_vit_l + avgpool + weighted + multiprofile) achieves 0.706 acc. The one major untested dimension is whether scgpt, a more expressive pre-trained scRNA-seq encoder, can surpass scvi_n200. Question: Does scgpt outperform scvi_n200 as the RNA encoder in the best-performing multimodal pipeline? Why this experiment: config_049 changes exactly one variable (RNA encoder: scvi_n200→scgpt) from the current best (config_037), keeping the validated optimal image encoder (imagenet_vit_l), aggregation (avgpool), fusion (weighted), and profile setting (multiprofile) fixed. This is the highest-information single experiment: if scgpt ≥ scvi_n200 it identifies a new best configuration; if scgpt < scvi_n200 it confirms scvi_n200 as the optimal RNA encoder and closes the last major design dimension, allowing focus on finer-tuning. Under a limited budget, resolving the RNA encoder question in the strongest pipeline is more valuable than testing secondary dimensions (e.g., transformer aggregation, which already showed inferior performance)."
}

